---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PrimeFactorization from '@/components/tools/calc2/PrimeFactorization.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="prime-factorization-calculator">
  <PrimeFactorization client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the prime factorization calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The prime factorization calculator divides your number by the smallest prime that goes into it,
      then divides the quotient again, until only a prime is left. The primes it divided by are the
      prime factors. Repeated factors are collected into powers:
    </p>
    <pre><code>n = p₁^e₁ × p₂^e₂ × … × pₖ^eₖ
number of divisors = (e₁ + 1)(e₂ + 1) … (eₖ + 1)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Every whole number above 1 has exactly one prime factorization, apart from the order of the
      factors. This is the fundamental theorem of arithmetic, and it is why a factor tree always ends
      with the same primes, whichever split you start with.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example: the prime factors of 360</h2>
    <pre><code>360 ÷ 2 = 180
180 ÷ 2 = 90
 90 ÷ 2 = 45
 45 ÷ 3 = 15
 15 ÷ 3 = 5     (5 is prime)
360 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 5 = 2³ × 3² × 5</code></pre>
    <p>
      360 has (3 + 1)(2 + 1)(1 + 1) = 24 divisors. Each divisor picks 0 to 3 twos, 0 to 2 threes and 0 or
      1 five.
    </p>

    <h2>Large numbers</h2>
    <p>
      Up to 10¹⁸ the calculator divides by the primes up to 1,000, tests what is left with the Miller-Rabin
      test and splits a large composite with Pollard’s rho method. 999,999,943,999,999,559 = 999,999,937 ×
      1,000,000,007 takes well under a second. Trial division alone would need about 50 million divisions
      for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Above 10¹⁸ the work moves to parallel WebAssembly workers. They run the elliptic curve
      method: many independent attempts, and the first core to find a factor stops the others. Its time
      depends on the size of the factor, not of the number. 2²⁵⁶ − 1 has 78 digits and takes a moment,
      because its largest factor is small for its size.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Whole numbers from 2 up to 300 digits. 0 and 1 have no prime factorization.</li>
      <li>
        Two prime factors of more than about 35 digits each can take hours. You can stop at any time and
        keep the factors found so far.
      </li>
      <li>Above about 3.3 × 10²⁴, a “prime” factor passes 33 rounds of Miller-Rabin, which is a test and not a proof.</li>
      <li>The factor tree shows up to 13 leaves one prime at a time. With more, it takes out whole prime powers in each split.</li>
      <li>The list of divisors appears when there are 64 or fewer; the count is always shown.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
